1. b3 d5 2. Bb2 Nf6 3. e3 g6 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. h3 O-O 6. g4 c5 7. Bg2 Nc6 8. d3 Bd7
9. Nbd2 Qc7 10. Nf1 Rfd8 11. Ng3 Nxg4 12. hxg4 Bxb2 13. Rb1 Bc3+ 14. Nd2 Ne5
15. Bf3 Qa5 16. Ngf1 Qxa2 17. Be2 Qa5 18. f4 Nc6 19. Kf2 Qc7 20. Bf3 d4 21. Ne4
dxe3+ 22. Kg3 Bg7 23. Nxe3 Be8 24. Qc1 ({Some sources give} 24. Qf1 {but all
the 1920s sources that Brian Denman and I have seen give 24.Qc1. However,
Brian makes this interesting observation: "I came across the score of the game
about thirty years ago in a 1947 book by Reinfeld about British Masters past
and present. I transcribed the game with 24 Qf1 and the author gives a diagram
showing the queen on that square. Only when I read your recent note in
BritBase did I think to query 24 Qf1. I then started looking at 1920s sources
and they all quoted 24 Qc1. However, looking at the position, White does not
want to play 24 Nxc5 because of 24...Bd4. I assume therefore that 24 Qc1 was
to threaten Nxc5, as the knight on e3 will be defended. However, if White had
this plan, 24 Qd2 would have been a more logical move, and, as he did not see
Black's queen sacrifice, we might ask why he did not carry through his
apparent plan by capturing on c5 after Black's 24th move. Instead White played
25 c3??. Now I know this misses the mate, but it also leaves the 'd' pawn en
prise. On the other hand 24 Qf1 appears to have the plan of 25 c3, as it
defends the d3 square. The queen on f1 might also hope to go to h3. My
instincts are telling me that White played 24 Qf1, but there is no way of
proving this and the sources seem to suggest otherwise." Brian makes a very
strong case for 24 Qf1, despite the older sources. I've since found 24 Qf1
also given in '500 Master Games of Chess' by Tartakower & du Mont (Bell, 1952).
I'm wondering whether the early reporters simply got the score wrong, and then
perhaps one of the players later pointed this out in print and subsequent
authors became aware of an emendation to the score. Who knows? - JS}) 24... h6
25. c3 Qxf4+ 26. Kf2 Ne5 {Sources: Yorkshire Post and Leeds Intelligencer, 4
January 1928; BCM, Feb 1928, p65 & BCM, Nov 1953, p309; Illustrated London
News, 19 May 1928} 0-1

