1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. c4 d5 4. Nc3 Nbd7 5. Bg5 c6 6. e3 Qa5 7. Nd2 Bb4 8. Qc2
O-O 9. Bh4 Re8 10. Nb3 Qc7 11. Bd3 dxc4 12. Bxc4 Nd5 13. e4 Nxc3 14. bxc3 Be7
15. Bg3 e5 16. O-O Bd6 17. Rad1 Nf8 18. dxe5 Bxe5 19. f4 Bd6 20. e5 Be7 21. f5
Qb6+ 22. Kh1 Ba3 23. f6 gxf6 24. exf6 Be6 25. Qd2 {(The annotator gives this
an exclamation mark; I'd be more inclined to give it two question marks) "The
thin trap of BxB is thus laid, but Black is alive to the danger which follows
White's Q-R6."} Ng6 ({I (JS) am less alive to the danger. Is there any? Why not
} 25... Bxc4 {, to answer} 26. Qh6 {with} ({and if} 26. Qg5+ {, to force the
knight to block with} Ng6 {, then} 27. Qh6 Bf8 {is a complete defence}) 26...
Ne6 {and I'm not convinced White can give mate. If} 27. Rd4 {Black can reply}
Bf8 {or even snaffle the rook on f1}) 26. Bf2 Qd8 27. Qxd8 Rexd8 28. Rxd8+ Rxd8
29. Bxe6 fxe6 30. f7+ Kg7 31. Bxa7 Bf8 ({"To prevent 32.Nc5 shutting off the B,
" writes the annotator. Which makes little or no sense. The text move is
disastrous; instead} 31... Rd7 {would at least keep the game going - JS}) 32.
Nd4 Ra8 33. Nxe6+ Kh6 34. Be3+ {Source: Western Morning News, 7 March 1928 
(patronising comments supplied by JS)} 1-0

